{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Heights and Distances","count":3,"questions":[{"id":51699,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Heights and Distances","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A sphere with centre $\\mathrm{O}$ sits atop a pole as shown in the figure. An observer on the ground is at a distance $50 \\mathrm{ m}$ from the foot of the pole. She notes that the angles of elevation from the observer to points $\\mathrm{P}$ and $\\mathrm{Q}$ on the sphere are $30^{{}^{\\circ}}$ and $60^{{}^{\\circ}}$, respectively. Then, the radius of the sphere in meters is -\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FYoVtbUBQPkR4Mg7RUjEdhhI9T_G_mYdAqKeBWhghG0Q.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FYoVtbUBQPkR4Mg7RUjEdhhI9T_G_mYdAqKeBWhghG0Q.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FYoVtbUBQPkR4Mg7RUjEdhhI9T_G_mYdAqKeBWhghG0Q.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$100 \\l left(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)$","$\\frac{50 \\sqrt{6}}{3}$","$50 \\l left(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)$","$\\frac{100 \\sqrt{6}}{3}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fx1ih_-qUsrgEpTCKL5N1lp09jirR9Fg6ooXa9eZY_EE.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fx1ih_-qUsrgEpTCKL5N1lp09jirR9Fg6ooXa9eZY_EE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fx1ih_-qUsrgEpTCKL5N1lp09jirR9Fg6ooXa9eZY_EE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n$\\mathrm{DE}=\\mathrm{BC}=\\mathrm{r}\\tan 30^{{}^{\\circ}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{h}}{50}\\mathrm{ h}=\\frac{50}{\\sqrt{3}}\\tan 60^{{}^{\\circ}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{h}+\\mathrm{r}}{50-\\mathrm{r}}\\sqrt{3}(50-\\mathrm{r})=\\mathrm{h}+\\mathrm{r}\\sqrt{3}(50-\\mathrm{r})=\\frac{50}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\mathrm{r}3(50-\\mathrm{r})=50+\\sqrt{3} \\mathrm{r}100=(3+\\sqrt{3}) \\mathrm{r}r=\\frac{100}{3+\\sqrt{3}}r=\\frac{100(3-\\sqrt{3})}{6}=50 \\l left(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213371978653bdef8c2"},{"id":51700,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Heights and Distances","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A wall is inclined to the floor at an angle of $135^{{}^{\\circ}}$. A ladder of length $\\ell$ is resting on the wall. As the ladder slides down, its mid-point traces an arc of an ellipse. Then the area of the ellipse is\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fg2s6jkRQqhifPzXQHbbY3mGffnjMVb1er6kP8qYzPtA.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fg2s6jkRQqhifPzXQHbbY3mGffnjMVb1er6kP8qYzPtA.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fg2s6jkRQqhifPzXQHbbY3mGffnjMVb1er6kP8qYzPtA.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$\\frac{\\pi \\ell^{2}}{4}$","$\\pi \\ell^{2}$","$4 \\pi \\ell^{2}$","$2 \\pi \\ell^{2}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FvS2ht1M3KC9BTOJjuylYPM0PokF2vD1n9iDGsFpCcIw.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FvS2ht1M3KC9BTOJjuylYPM0PokF2vD1n9iDGsFpCcIw.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FvS2ht1M3KC9BTOJjuylYPM0PokF2vD1n9iDGsFpCcIw.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nMid point $(\\mathrm{h}, \\mathrm{k})=\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{x}-\\mathrm{x}_{1}}{2}, \\frac{\\mathrm{x}_{1}}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{Now}\\left(\\mathrm{x}+\\mathrm{x}_{1}\\right)^{2}+\\mathrm{x}_{1}^{2}=\\ell^{2}$\nAs $2 \\mathrm{ h}+4 \\mathrm{k}=\\mathrm{x}+\\mathrm{x}_{1}, 2 \\mathrm{k}=\\mathrm{x}$,\nSo required locus is $\\begin{array}{l}4(\\mathrm{ h}+2 \\mathrm{k})^{2}+4 \\mathrmk^{2}=\\ell^{2} \\\\\n\\mathrm{ h}^{2}+5 \\mathrmk^{2}+4 \\mathrm{hk}=\\frac{\\ell^{2}}{4}$\\\\\n$\\mathrmx^{2}+5 \\mathrmy^{2}+4 \\mathrm{xy}=\\frac{\\ell^{2}}{4}\n\\end{array}$ Whose area is $\\frac{\\pi \\ell^{2}}{4}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213eba4acb4ea758374"},{"id":51701,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Heights and Distances","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A person standing on the top of a building of height $60\\sqrt{3}$ feet observed the top of a tower to lie at an elevation of $45 °.$ That person descended to the bottom of the building and found that the top of the same tower is now at an angle of elevation of $60 °.$& The height of the tower (in feet) is","options":["$30$","$30 ( \\sqrt{3} + 1 )$","$90 ( \\sqrt{3} + 1 )$","$150 ( \\sqrt{3} + 1 )$"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>$\\dfrac{60\\sqrt{3} + d}{d} = \\sqrt{3}$<br />\n$⇒ d = \\dfrac{60\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3} - 1}$</p> <p>$⇒ h = 60\\sqrt{3} \\left(1 + \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3} - 1}\\right)</p> <p>$= $\\dfrac{60 \\times 3}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} =90 ( \\sqrt{3} +1 )$</p> <p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fcc0cdb5d-b988-4aa1-8e4a-8e9c1ddb420e-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fcc0cdb5d-b988-4aa1-8e4a-8e9c1ddb420e-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fcc0cdb5d-b988-4aa1-8e4a-8e9c1ddb420e-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 188px;\" /></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da7503e9ae2d193c17303"}]}